.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt NQCHK 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqchk
.Nd check a capsule folder against its seal
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fo nqchk
.Fa "const char *dir"
.Fa "const uint8_t *trust" "size_t trustlen"
.Fa "nqrep **rep"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nqchk
checks the capsule in the folder
.Fa dir
.Pq Xr noqtis-capsule 5
and stores in
.Fa *rep
a report with one row per check
.Pq Xr nqrep 3 .
It runs every check it can, rather than stopping at the first failure,
so the report says everything that is wrong.
.Pp
It reads
.Pa noqtis.sig
(at most 1 MiB, never through a link) and checks that it is canonical JSON
with exactly the seal's fields; reads
.Pa noqtis.json
(at most 512 MiB; the size is checked before reading and again if the file
grows while it is read) and checks that its SHA-256 is the seal's
.Ql manifest_sha256 ;
verifies the signature over the exact manifest bytes with the seal's
algorithm and context; lists the folder; and reads the manifest one entry
at a time in its canonical layout, checking each listed file (present, a
regular file, size, SHA-256, SHA3-256) as it goes, the sort order and
duplicates.
Finally it reports every regular file present but not listed, every link,
special file or refused name, and listed paths that differ only in case.
.Pp
.Fa trust
is the public key the capsule must be sealed with,
.Fa trustlen
bytes in the algorithm's encoding: the seal's key must equal it, and the
signature is verified with it.
With
.Fa trust
.Dv NULL
(and
.Fa trustlen
0), the key inside the seal is used, and the report's
.Ql key
row says the key was not pinned: the capsule is then shown unchanged
since it was sealed, but not who sealed it.
.Pp
.Fn nqchk
is
.Xr nqchkt 3
with no TSA trust anchors.
A seal that carries a timestamp therefore fails its
.Ql timestamp
row here (with the imprint still checked against the signature): a
timestamp that cannot be checked is never passed silently.
.Pp
Memory use is the manifest bytes once (the signature covers them, so
they must be in memory to be verified), the folder listing (about 100
bytes per file), the report (one row per file) and constant state per
entry; the manifest is never parsed into a tree.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Bl -tag -width NQEVRFY
.It Dv NQOK
Every check passed.
.Fa *rep
holds the report.
.It Bq Er NQEVRFY
At least one check failed;
.Fa *rep
still holds the report, which says which.
.It Bq Er NQEARG
.Fa dir
or
.Fa rep
is
.Dv NULL ,
or
.Fa trust
is
.Dv NULL
with a nonzero
.Fa trustlen .
No report.
.It Bq Er NQEMEM
The allocation hook returned
.Dv NULL .
No report.
.El
.Pp
With no report,
.Fa *rep
is set to
.Dv NULL .
Free a report with
.Fn nqrepfree
.Pq Xr nqrep 3 .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nqrep *rep = NULL;
int rc = nqchk("results", pk, sizeof pk, &rep);
if (rc == NQOK)
  puts("unchanged since sealed by that key");
else if (rc == NQEVRFY)
  puts("changed or not sealed by that key: see the report");
nqrepfree(rep);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-check 1 ,
.Xr nqchkt 3 ,
.Xr nqrep 3 ,
.Xr nqseal 3 ,
.Xr noqtis-capsule 5
